{
  "dom_id": "step-q51130",
  "name": "Question 19",
  "problem": "The following table shows the speed $v$, in $\\text{km/min},$ at certain times during a family car trip over a period of $60$ minutes.\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{min})$ | $60$ | $70$ | $80$ | $95$ | $110$ | $120$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $v\\,(\\text{km/min})$ | $1.5$ | $2$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1.5$ |\n\nAssuming that the family traveled $130\\,\\text{km}$ during the first hour of the trip, use the left Riemann sum to approximate the total distance traveled during the first two hours.\n\n*Hint: The distance traveled is given by the area under the speed-time graph.*",
  "worked_solution": "In this case, we have $n=5$ rectangles. Using the left Riemann sum, the area is approximated as\n\n$$\nA≈v(60)Δ{t}_{1}+v(70)Δ{t}_{2}+v(80)Δ{t}_{3}+v(95)Δ{t}_{4}+v(110)Δ{t}_{5},\n$$\n\nwhere each $Δ{t}_{i}$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $t$-value and the next one, i.e.,\n\n$$\nΔ{t}_{i}={t}_{i+1}-{t}_{i}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $Δt$'s.\n\n| $t$ | $60$ | $70$ | $80$ | $95$ | $110$ | $120$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Δt$ | $10$ | $10$ | $15$ | $15$ | $10$ | $-$ |\n| $v(t)$ | $1.5$ | $2$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1.5$ |\n\nFinally, we plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈10\\cdot 1.5+10\\cdot 2+15\\cdot 1+15\\cdot 1+10\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =15+20+15+15+20 \\\\  & =85\\,\\text{km}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the given context, the area under the curve represents the distance covered in $60$ minutes of travel, from $t=60$ minutes to $t=120$ minutes. The distance traveled in the first two hours is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d=130+85=215\\,\\text{km}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
